    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "description",
      "value": "Culture and religion have usually been seen as closely related. Paul Tillich looked at religion as the soul of culture and culture as the form or framework of religion.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0045",
        "chunk_title": "Culture + Criticism",
        "chunk_order": 44,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "quote",
      "value": "Religion as ultimate concern is the meaning-giving substance of culture, and culture is the totality of forms in which the basic concern of religion expresses itself. In abbreviation: religion is the substance of culture, culture is the form of religion.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0045",
        "chunk_title": "Culture + Criticism",
        "chunk_order": 44,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "theorist",
      "value": "Ernst Troeltsch",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0045",
        "chunk_title": "Culture + Criticism",
        "chunk_order": 44,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "theory",
      "value": "Culture as the soil of religion; transplanting a religion from its original culture to a foreign culture would kill it.",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "source": null,
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_id": "page_0045",
        "chunk_title": "Culture + Criticism",
        "chunk_order": 44,
        "source_type": "page"
      }
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "field": "arguments",
      "value": "Many attempts in the modern pluralistic situation to distinguish culture from religion.",
